WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.920
51 trials showed drugs and lifestyle lowered epigenetic age markers while supplements did not. This is Planetarian Daily Episode 244. A T-cell enzyme in mice, a bacterial switch that opens barriers, and a deep dive on forced smiles come after that. Yale School of Medicine, according to Science Daily, pooled clinical studies that tracked DNA methylation biomarkers used to estimate biological age. More than 110 of those biomarkers went into the analysis.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.360
and 16 major epigenetic clocks sat among them.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:38.020
The trials measured methylation patterns before each intervention and again after it, giving

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:39.580
every clock a paired reading.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.780
All of that data came from blood samples, which were run through established epigenetic

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:45.740
clock algorithms.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.380
Lead author Raghav Segal noted that the work marks the first demonstration that certain

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.940
therapies produce measurable shifts in these markers.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:00.774
Mouse models in which a T cell enzyme was selectively deleted, reported in immunity, showed

00:01:00.774 --> 00:01:02.874
slower aging across multiple organs.

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:06.294
Immune function remained intact in the modified animals.

00:01:06.974 --> 00:01:11.574
Organes in the deficient animals showed reduced age-related decline compared with controls,

00:01:12.074 --> 00:01:15.274
so organ aging slowed while the immune system still worked.

00:01:16.146 --> 00:01:21.666
Pendental medicine scientists, according to Fizz.org, mapped a previously unknown molecular

00:01:21.666 --> 00:01:26.926
switch that lets bacteria rapidly compromise epithelial defenses. The mechanism

00:01:26.926 --> 00:01:30.966
allows pathogens to alter host cell junctions without triggering immediate

00:01:30.966 --> 00:01:36.266
detection. That switch acts through a previously unrecognized pathway, which is why

00:01:36.266 --> 00:01:40.626
the breach can begin before an immune alarm sounds. Experiments showed that

00:01:40.626 --> 00:01:45.346
blocking the mechanism preserve barrier function in cell and tissue models. The

00:01:45.346 --> 00:01:48.986
work identifies a potential target for interventions that preserve barrier

00:01:48.986 --> 00:01:54.326
integrity during early infection, aimed at host side protection rather than direct bacterial

00:01:54.326 --> 00:01:54.786
killing.

00:01:55.226 --> 00:02:00.066
One open question is whether the same pathway operates across different epithelial surfaces

00:02:00.066 --> 00:02:01.086
in living animals.

00:02:02.072 --> 00:02:07.792
Intestinal bacteria produced a compound that, according to medical express, increased nicotinamide

00:02:07.792 --> 00:02:11.892
adenine dinucleotide or NAD in models of gut injury.

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:15.532
The same molecule restored tissue integrity after the injury.

00:02:15.532 --> 00:02:20.052
The effect ran through direct metabolic support rather than immune modulation.

00:02:20.752 --> 00:02:24.972
Researchers traced that activity to a specific bacterial biosynthetic pathway.

00:02:26.004 --> 00:02:32.144
Elevated serotonin output from a defined brain region, reported by lifespan.io, suppressed

00:02:32.144 --> 00:02:33.804
dopamine release in older mice.

00:02:34.304 --> 00:02:37.344
That drop in dopamine went with lower voluntary movement.

00:02:37.784 --> 00:02:41.884
Selective ablation of the serotonin-producing cells restored activity levels.

00:02:41.884 --> 00:02:46.684
The study identifies a neural circuit that contributes to age-related inactivity.

00:02:47.044 --> 00:02:52.263
Most people assume that deliberately smiling will lift their mood, yet University of Florida

00:02:52.263 --> 00:02:56.383
testing covered by science daily found the opposite when the action was repeated.

00:02:56.963 --> 00:03:02.443
More than 1,100 participants completed tasks that required them to smile either once or

00:03:02.443 --> 00:03:04.403
repeatedly under controlled conditions.

00:03:04.843 --> 00:03:09.303
A single artificial smile produced a small positive shift in self-reported mood.

00:03:09.303 --> 00:03:14.683
Repeat the action, erase that benefit, or reverse it, leaving people below baseline.

00:03:15.183 --> 00:03:19.483
The pattern held after the team controlled for each person's initial emotional state.

00:03:20.425 --> 00:03:24.985
The mechanism appears tied to the brain registering a mismatch between the expression and the

00:03:24.985 --> 00:03:29.785
underlying feeling, and that mismatch grows more salient each time the smile is forced

00:03:29.785 --> 00:03:30.145
again.

00:03:30.765 --> 00:03:34.825
Performed only once, simple motor feedback can still nudge affect upward.

00:03:34.825 --> 00:03:40.045
kept going, the repeated mismatch registers as inauthentic and subtracts from mood.

00:03:40.685 --> 00:03:43.925
The briefing places that pattern in ordinary settings such as meetings,

00:03:44.285 --> 00:03:48.825
commutes, and family photos, where a smile is often held longer than a single beat.

00:03:49.525 --> 00:03:53.385
Further trials that varied the duration and social context of forced smiling

00:03:53.385 --> 00:03:56.345
would clarify how widely the reversal applies.

00:03:57.427 --> 00:04:02.227
Climate-oriented agricultural papers in a Frontiers Review carried by Fizz.org

00:04:02.227 --> 00:04:06.447
work, describe partnerships and institutional links often in project write-ups.

00:04:06.887 --> 00:04:10.907
Those social relationships are seldom measured against actual scaling outcomes.

00:04:11.507 --> 00:04:16.347
The author's examined how such ties influence whether innovation spread beyond pilot sites.

00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:21.027
They conclude that explicit documentation of the relationships would improve monitoring

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:22.987
and evaluation of scaling efforts.

00:04:24.009 --> 00:04:28.689
Florida International University tested common classroom accommodations in students with

00:04:28.689 --> 00:04:32.069
tension deficit hyperactivity disorder, or ADHD.

00:04:32.749 --> 00:04:36.909
The supports included extended time, frequent breaks, and quiet workspaces.

00:04:37.489 --> 00:04:41.869
None of those supports produced measurable gains in task completion or accuracy relative

00:04:41.869 --> 00:04:42.929
to control conditions.

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:47.909
Performance was tracked on standardized academic measures across multiple sessions.

00:04:48.862 --> 00:04:54.242
A Tsukuba team isolated short peptides from the Drosophila-Nemory protein, a fruit-fly

00:04:54.242 --> 00:04:55.102
sleep protein.

00:04:55.102 --> 00:04:58.502
Those peptides killed drug-resistant bacteria in culture.

00:04:58.942 --> 00:05:03.022
They retained activity against strains that had already developed resistance to conventional

00:05:03.022 --> 00:05:03.722
antibiotics.

00:05:04.302 --> 00:05:10.344
The work was published in Nature Communications, circulating NAD in more than 1,000 adults,

00:05:10.584 --> 00:05:15.784
according to medical express, tracked cardiovascular disease status more closely than chronological

00:05:15.784 --> 00:05:16.224
age.

00:05:17.144 --> 00:05:21.764
Participants with established heart disease displayed lower NAD independent of their birth year.

00:05:21.764 --> 00:05:26.564
The association persisted after statistical adjustment for common risk factors.

00:05:26.864 --> 00:05:32.454
Comparative genomic analysis, according to Fizz.org, revealed sequence changes that

00:05:32.454 --> 00:05:36.294
increase progenitor cell proliferation in the developing primate cortex.

00:05:36.994 --> 00:05:40.454
The alterations differ from previously known human-specific variants.

00:05:40.934 --> 00:05:43.794
They still produce comparable expansion of cortical layers.

00:05:44.294 --> 00:05:48.994
The work examined multiple primate species to trace the evolutionary timing of the changes.

00:05:48.994 --> 00:05:54.875
rapid mountain-building pulses in a science-advances paper coincided with major

00:05:54.875 --> 00:05:59.375
increases in fossil diversity in two distinct episodes. The author's link

00:05:59.375 --> 00:06:03.415
heightened erosion and nutrient runoff from the new ranges to expanded marine

00:06:03.415 --> 00:06:08.515
and terrestrial habitats. The correlation spans the Proterozoic and Phanerozoic

00:06:08.515 --> 00:06:14.297
eons. In brief, Union Minister Jitendra Singh reported that India's bioeconomy

00:06:14.297 --> 00:06:19.577
has grown from roughly $10 billion to more than $195 billion during the

00:06:19.577 --> 00:06:24.897
current government's tenure, with biotechnology startups rising from between 50 and 60 to

00:06:24.897 --> 00:06:29.917
over 11,000, as a center called for stronger industry participation in the country's

00:06:29.917 --> 00:06:31.437
biotechnology ecosystem.

00:06:32.137 --> 00:06:36.917
Researchers in Russia have begun longitudinal observations of aged primates to investigate

00:06:36.917 --> 00:06:42.597
factors that might extend healthy lifespan, as Putin reportedly eyes a 150-year lifespan.

00:06:42.597 --> 00:06:49.597
The 5th International Symposium on Citrus Biotechnology, hosted by NRIE in Corsica, will

00:06:49.597 --> 00:06:52.517
